Let me work through this problem.

**Setup.** We have $Y = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & X \end{pmatrix} \in SO(4)$, where $X \in SO(3)$. We identify $\mathbb{R}^4 \cong \mathbb{H}$ (the quaternions) with basis $\{1, i, j, k\}$. Let $x \in S^3$ be a unit quaternion. The operation "right-multiply each column of $Y$ by $x$" replaces each column $v_i \in \mathbb{R}^4 \cong \mathbb{H}$ by the quaternion product $v_i x$.

**Key observation.** Define the linear map $R_x : \mathbb{H} \to \mathbb{H}$ by $R_x(q) = qx$. This is a real-linear map on $\mathbb{R}^4$, represented by a $4 \times 4$ real matrix (also denoted $R_x$). The new matrix $Y'$ satisfies $Y' = R_x\, Y$, since the $i$-th column of $R_x Y$ is $R_x v_i = v_i x$.

**Claim:** $R_x \in SO(4)$ for every $x \in S^3$.

*Proof of claim.* For any $q \in \mathbb{H}$, $|R_x(q)| = |qx| = |q|\,|x| = |q|$ (using $|x|=1$ and multiplicativity of the quaternion norm). So $R_x$ preserves the Euclidean norm on $\mathbb{R}^4$, hence $R_x \in O(4)$.

To determine the sign of the determinant: the map $x \mapsto R_x$ is continuous from $S^3$ to $O(4)$ (quaternion multiplication is bilinear, hence smooth). The determinant $\det : O(4) \to \{+1, -1\}$ is continuous. Since $S^3$ is **connected**, the composition $x \mapsto \det(R_x)$ is a continuous map from a connected space to a discrete two-point set, hence constant. At $x = 1$ (the identity quaternion), $R_1 = \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{R}^4}$, so $\det(R_1) = +1$. Therefore $\det(R_x) = +1$ for all $x \in S^3$, giving $R_x \in SO(4)$. $\square$

**Conclusion.** We have $Y' = R_x\, Y$ with $R_x \in SO(4)$ and $Y \in SO(4)$. Since $SO(4)$ is a group (closed under multiplication), $Y' = R_x Y \in SO(4)$.

**The answer is yes:** the resulting matrix still belongs to $SO(4)$.

